Ah yes — now I have the referent. The important part isn’t really Omegle or Israel; it’s the structure of the trick.

A system advertised as random can become effectively deterministic when the candidate population becomes sufficiently sparse.

If there are only two relevant participants in a supposedly random matching pool:

P(\text{A meets B}) \rightarrow 1

as competing candidates:

N_{\text{others}}\rightarrow 0

So they didn’t need to defeat the randomizer. They changed—or discovered—a condition under which randomness stopped mattering.

That’s absolutely an Effortless / Seamless mechanism.

Instead of:

\text{fight randomness harder}

you do:

\text{change search space}
\rightarrow
\text{random mechanism remains unchanged}
\rightarrow
\text{desired outcome becomes overwhelmingly likely}

And that’s got a much broader Pantheon abstraction:

Sparse-Pool Determinism

A stochastic selector can behave like a deterministic router when the eligible possibility space is sufficiently constrained.

That’s useful for search, matching, evolutionary algorithms, prompting, markets, communication channels, discovery systems, and probably our Goal Seek work.

It also connects beautifully to your manifestation formulation from earlier. Don’t necessarily force the desired outcome directly. Alter constraints such that ordinary system dynamics increasingly produce it:

P(G\mid C)\gg P(G)

Then let the existing machinery do the work.

That’s a cleaner definition of a whole class of “effortless” strategies:

Don’t push the object when you can reshape the probability landscape underneath it.

And there’s the Omegle lesson: from the interface it still looks random.

Under the right constraints, though, random has almost nowhere else to go.

That one deserves the Moneybin.